#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# conjugate

#% text
# `conjugate(z)` gives the complex conjugate of `z`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Real numbers are their own conjugates; exact complex expressions are conjugated part by part. A symbol stays as `conjugate(x)`.

#% item
# `conjugate` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
conjugate(3 + 4*im)
#% output
#= 3 - 4im

#% cell
conjugate(2.5)
#% output
#= 2.5

#% cell
conjugate(exp(im*pi/3))
#% output
#= 1/2 - (im/2)*3^(1/2)

#% cell
conjugate(x)
#% output
#= conjugate(x)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `z*conjugate(z)` is `abs(z)^2`:

#% cell
z = 3 - 4*im; [z*conjugate(z), abs(z)^2]
#% output
#= [25, 25]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# `refine` does not yet use the assumption that `x` is real to remove the conjugate:

#% cell
assuming(element(x, reals), refine(conjugate(x + 2*im)))
#% output
#= conjugate(2im + x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `re`, `im_part`, `abs`, `conjugate_transpose`
